Request:
Migration parsing fails for Zig-embedded SQL migrations due to newline handling. I'm embedding many small .sql files inside a Zig array using @embedFile, then iterating to run them. Example code:

const std = @import("std");

const migrations = []struct { name: []const u8, sql: []const u8 } {
    .{ "0001_init", @embedFile("../migrations/0001_init.sql") },
    .{ "0002_seed", @embedFile("../migrations/0002_seed.sql") },
};

pub fn run(allocator: *std.mem.Allocator) !void {
    var db = try DB.connect(...);
    for (m) in migrations {
        try db.exec(m.sql);
    }
}

Observed problem: some embedded SQL files end up with CRLF (
) and parser's split-on-semicolon logic considers \r as part of statement and fails to recognize ending semicolon. Error:

Error: SQLExecutionError { message: "syntax error at end of input", sql: "INSERT INTO ... VALUES ('x')\r" }

Proposed fix: normalize line endings when embedding or reading files: strip \r characters before splitting statements. Update embedding docs to mention CRLF normalization. Also add a unit test embedding a file with CRLF and ensure migrator handles it. Consider adding helper fn normalizeNewlines(s: []const u8) []const u8 to be applied to embedded content and file reads.

